WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.760
Now let's talk specifically about organizational libraries.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.710
Organizational libraries are libraries, just like jQuery, but instead of

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:14.740
focusing on AJAX and DOM manipulation, they focus on application organization.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:15.980
MVC or MVVM,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.970
what we know as model view octopus, and often some other things as well.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.990
Now, there are a lot of organizational libraries out there, but

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.650
they're all solving the same problem in fundamentally similar ways.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.040
They do have slight differences in organization or

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.540
implementation, and that's what the documentation is for.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.200
But by now, if you understand the basics of separation of concerns,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.830
then you can understand any of these organizational libraries.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.846
Now, what about frameworks?

00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:50.918
What is a framework, and how do they differ from organizational libraries?

00:00:50.918 --> 00:00:53.690
Well, while scripting this course, I talked to a bunch of

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.350
people about this question because I was a little fuzzy on it myself.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.290
And it turns out, everyone has a different answer and

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:05.480
none of them fit 100% with what libraries and frameworks call themselves.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.450
So let's take a step back.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.120
Here's the real issue.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.530
It's not one person or institution defining these words.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.330
It's a community of thousands and

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.470
thousands of developers, and they aren't all on the same page as each other.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.500
Some people say frameworks are collections of libraries.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.680
Some other people say that frameworks call view render methods,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.330
while the libraries require you to call them.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.210
There's a wide range of definitions, and

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.630
if you do want proof of this, do a quick Google search and start counting.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.470
In fact, if you look at the edit history for

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.050
the Wikipedia pages on JavaScript for libraries and

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.200
frameworks, you'll even see disagreement among the editors themselves.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.340
So, what is the difference between an organizational library and a framework?

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.120
Well, it feels like a cop-out, but I would say, don't worry about it too much.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.540
Organizational libraries and

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.370
frameworks both solve the same problem and in similar ways.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.170
So, to me at least, it doesn't matter a ton what we call it.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.870
It just matters that we can use them regardless of what they call themselves.

